#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148287} :activity/id #uuid "6cd22d89-74ae-5514-8d31-658810158855" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048291 :topic/id #uuid "91890390-2801-5fd4-8f87-a9c37f742926" :topic/math-academy-id 2525} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148746 :tutorial/content "**Damped harmonic motion** is a form of harmonic motion where there is an additional damping force proportional to the object's velocity, $y',$ and acts in the opposite direction to that of the motion. This damping force acts to decrease the object's amplitude over time.\n\nIf the **damping coefficient** is $b,$ then the equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' = - by' - \\omega^2 y \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad y'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nConcretely, if you take a simple harmonic oscillator and place it in a viscous fluid (e.g., air), the result will be a damped harmonic oscillator." :tutorial/id #uuid "92725021-d224-5ce8-927f-8e97c66ade9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4068 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f76ac548-c4d6-58a9-a7ff-c011a4e7386c" :step/math-academy-id 24278 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147372 :question/id #uuid "18371fa9-696a-5a3a-b7e6-400f14d2fe62" :question/math-academy-id "e-6724"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050824}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357720}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "6fe421a6-8381-535b-acdf-27529fb1c5b2" :step/math-academy-id 24686 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148315}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147373 :question/id #uuid "365ec1d4-0301-5dfe-a731-35f12ea4d3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357725}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"} :step/id #uuid "84eb5ffa-7746-5e86-a7b2-576422ad0e72" :step/math-academy-id 24687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147374 :question/id #uuid "0458947c-70c8-5f88-9e52-68c8950afd7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6726"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357728}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "f9fd238a-cae7-5cac-b54a-a6e5205541ba" :step/math-academy-id 24688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148306}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148306 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147375 :question/id #uuid "c93a18f3-6d09-5d88-8a90-434fc08d3874" :question/math-academy-id "e-6727"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393940}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"} :step/id #uuid "876d7761-31c7-56a1-a2bc-f3e0e05e03c5" :step/math-academy-id 24692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148764 :tutorial/content "There are three types of damped harmonic motion:\n\n- **Heavy damping** occurs when the damping force is so large that the object returns to the equilibrium position very slowly, without any oscillation.\n\n- **Critical damping** occurs when the damping force is just sufficient to allow the object to return to the equilibrium position in the shortest amount of time. There are no oscillations.\n\n- **Light damping** occurs when the damping force is light enough that the object passes beyond the equilibrium position. The object oscillates back and forth about the equilibrium position, but the amplitude of oscillation is gradually reduced over time.\n\n![](images/79/79991a25e69629a10f69389be85537f058f66b187f9241a28a5f892ada4b0d2d.png)\n\nGiven an equation that describes damped motion, we can determine the type of damping by computing the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping.\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping.\n\nIntuitively, $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping because the characteristic equation has two complex solutions, meaning that the general solution contains sinusoidal terms that are multiplied by an exponential decay term. This results in oscillations that decay in amplitude as $t$ increases.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping because the characteristic equation has two real solutions. In practice, both of these real solutions will be negative, leading to exponential decay in the amplitude of the oscillator as $t$ increases.\n\nFinally, the case $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping because the characteristic equation has exactly one real solution, meaning that the general solution can be written in terms of as a single exponential decay term $y(t) = (c_1 + c_2 t)e^{\\lambda t}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cb17cc59-7cc0-5c34-87ac-7663dbb08c95" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4166 :tutorial/title "Light, Critical, and Heavy Damping"} :step/id #uuid "3cedcfab-cfca-5783-a755-b96fcbd7b336" :step/math-academy-id 25337 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148765 :tutorial/content "Remember that, if an object of mass $m$ is attached to a spring with spring constant $k,$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nmy'' = -ky.\n$$\n\nIf there is an additional damping force with damping coefficient $c,$ then we have\n\n$$\nmy'' = -cy'-ky.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9ba41777-f1f4-5722-b629-eb8be1f7e53d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4167 :tutorial/title "The Equation of Motion for a Mass-Spring System with Damping"} :step/id #uuid "6e244fad-d7c8-5529-acf3-f465d0566799" :step/math-academy-id 25338 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148305}}] :activity/title "Damped Oscillators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147704}]}
